-#
-#  The COLOURS of the "Moss Status" cell of the Buchhaltung summaries
-#  (Sachkonten, Kostenstellen and Kreditoren). Ruby knows the word and the class
-#  name (Fin::BookkeepingHelper#moss_status_cell), this file knows what they
-#  look like.
-#
-#  EVERY view that shows such a cell must render it:
-#      = render "shared/wsjrdp/moss_status_styles"
-#  The guard emits the <style> only once per request, so rendering it from a
-#  page AND from a partial it embeds is harmless (same pattern as
-#  shared/wsjrdp/_moss_kind_styles).
-#
-#  The status QUALIFIES a row, it does not classify it: it appears in every
-#  single row of the list, so anything louder than a tint would drown the
-#  numbers next to it. Hence no badge, no fill, no bold and no marker glyph --
-#  a colour, with the word carrying the meaning on its own.
- return if @wsjrdp_moss_status_styles_emitted
- @wsjrdp_moss_status_styles_emitted = true
:css
  /* A muted green for aktiv; for inaktiv the theme's own secondary text colour,
     which is the grey the rest of the page mutes with (this Bootstrap build has
     no --bs-secondary-color, a 5.3 token, hence the concrete fallback). */
  .moss-status { white-space: nowrap; }
  .moss-status-active { color: #3b7d5b; }
  .moss-status-inactive { color: var(--bs-secondary-color, #6c757d); }
